WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.077
PowerShell scripts receive input from many places: parameters, files, environment variables, pipeline input, scheduled task arguments, configuration data, and external service responses.

2
00:00:13.077 --> 00:00:16.010
Internal does not automatically mean safe.

3
00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:23.222
A script may be called by another automation job, a help desk technician, a CI pipeline, or a monitoring tool.

4
00:00:23.222 --> 00:00:30.038
Defensive scripts treat input as untrusted until it is checked against the script's actual contract.

5
00:00:30.038 --> 00:00:34.635
Parameter validation attributes are a useful first line of defense.

6
00:00:34.635 --> 00:00:38.122
ValidateSet restricts a parameter to known values.

7
00:00:38.122 --> 00:00:43.512
ValidatePattern can enforce a format when a regular expression is appropriate.

8
00:00:43.512 --> 00:00:46.523
ValidateRange can constrain numeric input.

9
00:00:46.523 --> 00:00:50.169
Mandatory parameters make required values explicit.

10
00:00:50.169 --> 00:00:56.113
Type constraints help PowerShell convert and reject values before the script body runs.

11
00:00:56.113 --> 00:01:00.552
These features make valid use easier and invalid use more obvious.

12
00:01:00.552 --> 00:01:02.058
Safe defaults matter.

13
00:01:02.058 --> 00:01:10.934
A script that defaults to every server, every mailbox, every tenant, or every directory can do too much when a caller forgets a filter.

14
00:01:10.934 --> 00:01:18.860
Prefer narrow defaults, explicit confirmation for broad operations, and clear parameter names that describe scope.

15
00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:28.450
Where possible, use ShouldProcess and WhatIf support for scripts that make changes so operators can preview intended work before applying it.

16
00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:32.571
Avoid assumptions about interactive users or trusted input.

17
00:01:32.571 --> 00:01:38.436
Automation may run unattended, receive pipeline objects, or consume generated files.

18
00:01:38.436 --> 00:01:52.940
Validate not only type and format, but also meaning: does the user name exist, is the path inside the expected location, is the target environment allowed, and is the requested action appropriate for the caller's role.

19
00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:59.281
Strong parameters turn scripts from loose command wrappers into safer administrative tools.
